out.push(S('Stage ✓ · Unit 7 check'));
out.push(A('**A** **1** a _(for)_   **2** b _(from)_   **3** c _(establishes)_   **4** b _(claim)_   **5** c _(held)_   **6** a _(concession)_   **7** c _(will)_   **8** a _(robust)_'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Item 7 is the one that separates a class and it is not a vocabulary point: _a reader who holds the file **will** say_.** **_Will_ here is prediction about a present disposition and takes the bare infinitive**; **_would have_ and _will have_ both put the saying in the past, and the objection has not been made yet.** **Teach it with the four-second test beside it** — **the objector exists and has not spoken, which is exactly what _will say_ encodes.** ⚠ **And item 2 is the preposition after _follow_**: **_it does not follow from this that_**, where _of_ and _with_ are both produced by about a fifth of any class and neither is English.'));
out.push(A('**B  Listening** **1** B   **2** A   **3** C   **4** D   **5** B   **6** C'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Part 3 at C1 has four options and the fourth is almost always a true statement answering a different question, which is the third time this book has said so.** **Item 4 is built on it**: _they are usually shorter_ **is what everybody expects and is the opposite of what she says**, and _they contain new claims_ is true of some and is not her point. **The answer is D — longer and accepted faster — and it is the answer because she says the second half, not the first.** ⚠ **Item 2 is the attitude item and the hardest here.** **She does not say one sentence is the only error: she says it tells her the writer has not thought about who reads this, and that she then does not know where else they have not.** **Option A carries that and option C contradicts it** — **and candidates who have not heard the second half of her answer take C.**'));
